  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The nor'easter departs the region Tuesday.\n\nThe nor'easter continues to weaken as it tracks slowly \nnortheastward out of the Northeastern US.\n\nWinds have now weakened substantially compared to the past few\ndays, now only around 10 mph or less out of the NW. Still some \nspotty sprinkles early this morning, but coverage continues to  \ndwindle. Cloud coverage will linger through Tuesday with a few \nbreaks possible. That said, not expecting a wall-to-wall sunny \nday just yet.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Coastal flooding threat lowered in many areas but \nstill a concern along the back bays of New Jersey and Delaware.\n\nOverall, tidal flooding concerns continue to diminish as winds have \nshifted offshore. Additional flooding at high tides is expected to \nbe spotty minor for most locations. \n\nMinor flooding of back bays continues to affect Ocean County NJ\nand Sussex County DE. Given the offshore flow, water levels \nwill gradually recede from here. However, minor flooding is \nexpected to persist into Tuesday. A Coastal Flood Advisory is in\neffect until 2 PM Tuesday for these locations. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled weather possible Friday through\nSunday.\n\nA cold front is forecast to move across the area late Friday into \nFriday night. As it moves into the area, there will be a chance of \nshowers. However, as the vorticity impulse aloft weakens, the \nshowers may dissipate for many areas as the move eastward. However, \nadditional rain fall is expected to move back into the area Saturday \nnight into Sunday. An area of low pressure is forecast to develop \nalong the front as it stalls to our south Saturday, then move \nnorthward across the area through Sunday. Showers will increase \nSaturday night into Sunday as a couple of short wave/vorticity \nimpulses move into the area, along with the surface low\npressure.",
